      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "A migration inspector finishes his check at Elena's restaurant. He is very impressed. He says there are no illegal workers. They decide to eat at Claude Monet. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ваш ресторан будет отличным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы все делаем правильно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Большое спасибо вам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вы меня очень удивили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ни одного повара без документов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я привыкла работать по закону.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мне было очень приятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Пойдём в другой ресторан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тут есть Клод Моне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отлично, люблю французскую кухню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "К нам идёт проверка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Все без документов, вон!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ваш ресторан будет отличным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Your restaurant will be excellent.' the speaker uses the polite plural 'you' here alongside 'restaurant', keeping a formal or respectful tone. Elena answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы все делаем правильно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We do everything right.' Notice the word order: 'we' comes first, and 'right' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Большое спасибо вам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you very much.' Listen for the word meaning 'big' right alongside 'thank you' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вы меня очень удивили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You really surprised me.' Here 'to surprise' uses the plural past-tense ending, which also serves for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ни одного повара без документов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not a single cook without documents.' The key word here means 'alone', which the speaker combines with 'cook'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я привыкла работать по закону.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I got used to working by the law.' The first content word means 'I', and 'law' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мне было очень приятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It was very pleasant for me.' the speaker starts with 'to me' and pairs it with 'was' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Пойдём в другой ресторан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us go to another restaurant?' In Russian, 'let's go' leads into 'restaurant' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тут есть Клод Моне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is Claude Monet.' The key word here means 'here', which the speaker combines with 'there is'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отлично, люблю французскую кухню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excellent, I love French cuisine.' Here 'perfect' sets up the idea, while 'kitchen' comes at the end for emphasis. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "К нам идёт проверка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'An inspection is coming to us!' Listen for the word meaning 'us' right alongside 'goes' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Все без документов, вон!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everyone without documents, get out!' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'documents' straight to 'documents'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
